---
import Tool from '../../components/Tool.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  lang="en"
  heading="View the prompt and metadata of an AI image"
  title="View the Prompt of an AI Image (PNG Info) — PromptWipe"
  description="Read the prompt, settings, ComfyUI workflow, EXIF, XMP and C2PA data of an AI image, like PNG Info. Free, in your browser: nothing is uploaded."
>
  <p class="lead">
    Drop an image to read what it carries: the prompt and settings from AUTOMATIC1111, Forge, Fooocus or InvokeAI, a
    ComfyUI workflow, EXIF and GPS, XMP and Content Credentials. It works like the PNG Info tab, in your browser, and
    leaves your file unchanged. It reads metadata only: it does not detect invisible watermarks.
  </p>
  <Tool lang="en" />
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>How do I see the prompt of an AI image?</h2>
    <p>
      Drop the file above, click to choose it, or paste it. A short verdict comes first, then “What the image
      contained”, which opens with the prompt and generation settings. Each text is shown under the name of its key, with who wrote it when
      the key is known: <code>parameters</code> for AUTOMATIC1111, Forge or Fooocus, <code>prompt</code> and
      <code>workflow</code> for ComfyUI, <code>invokeai_metadata</code> for InvokeAI.
    </p>
    <p>
      Long texts get their own box with a Copy button, which copies the full text even when the page shows only the
      first 20,000 characters. JSON, such as a ComfyUI workflow, and texts over 2,000 characters start folded: click to
      open them. PNG, JPEG and WebP files up to 50 MB are read; AVIF, HEIC and GIF are not supported yet, and the tool
      tells you so.
    </p>

    <h2>What does each part of the report mean?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Prompt and generation settings</strong>: the text blocks of a PNG, such as the A1111 infotext (prompt,
        negative prompt, seed, sampler, model and LoRA hashes) or a ComfyUI workflow. When A1111 saves a JPEG or WebP,
        the same text sits in EXIF instead, under “User comment”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>EXIF</strong>: camera maker and model, software, dates, description, and the GPS position as latitude
        and longitude when there is one. A maker note or an embedded thumbnail is listed with its size only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>XMP</strong>: the tool that created the file and its digital source type. The IPTC vocabulary uses
        <code>trainedAlgorithmicMedia</code> for “created using generative AI” and
        <code>compositeWithTrainedAlgorithmicMedia</code> for an image edited with it, such as an inpainted photo. A
        description, a credit or a link to Content Credentials can appear here too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Content Credentials (C2PA)</strong>: how many manifests the file holds and their size, the tool it says
        it was made with, and the signer as declared. PromptWipe does not check the signature; the Content Authenticity
        Initiative’s <a href="https://verify.contentauthenticity.org/">Verify</a> site does.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other data</strong>: an HDR gain map, secondary images such as a depth map, the short video of a Motion
        Photo, or data hidden after the end of the image.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Below come “What was removed” and “What was kept”, then a folded list of every block of the file in order, with
      its position and size, for the curious.
    </p>

    <h2>Can I read a ComfyUI workflow without opening ComfyUI?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. ComfyUI stores two JSON texts in its PNG files: <code>prompt</code>, the graph as it ran, and
      <code>workflow</code>, the graph as you see it on the canvas. The report shows both, folded, with the number of
      nodes in the workflow. Copy them into a text editor to read the node settings, the model and LoRA file names, and
      every prompt in the graph. The page on <a href="/remove-comfyui-workflow-from-image/">removing the ComfyUI
      workflow</a> explains what it reveals.
    </p>

    <h2>Does viewing the metadata change my file?</h2>
    <p>
      No. Your browser reads a copy of the file in memory; the file on your disk is never touched. PromptWipe also
      prepares a cleaned copy and offers it as a separate download, named after your file with <code>-clean</code>
      added. If you only wanted to read, ignore it. Nothing is stored: close the tab and the image is gone from the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Why does my image show no metadata?</h2>
    <p>Usually because it was removed before the image reached you. Common reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Social networks and messaging apps often re-encode uploaded images and drop their metadata on the way. A file
        saved from such a site has often lost its prompt already.
      </li>
      <li>
        A pasted image often loses its metadata on the way to the clipboard. Drop or choose the original file instead.
      </li>
      <li>A screenshot carries none of the original file’s metadata.</li>
      <li>
        The tool did not write any. A1111 has a setting to turn its infotext off, ComfyUI has a server option that
        stops it saving the workflow, and Fooocus saves metadata only when you turn it on.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An empty report does not mean the image was not made with AI. Invisible watermarks such as SynthID are part of
      the pixels, not of the metadata, and PromptWipe neither detects nor removes them.
    </p>

    <h2>Is it the same as the PNG Info tab?</h2>
    <p>
      Same idea, different place. AUTOMATIC1111’s PNG Info tab reads the infotext inside the web UI and can send the
      settings back to txt2img. PromptWipe needs nothing installed and also names ComfyUI, InvokeAI and Fooocus keys,
      lists EXIF fields, XMP and Content Credentials, and shows every block of the file. When you decide to share the
      image, the same page removes all of it: see <a href="/">remove metadata from AI images</a> and the page on
      <a href="/remove-stable-diffusion-metadata/">Stable Diffusion metadata</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Is my image uploaded to read it?</h2>
    <p>
      No. The reading happens in the page itself. The site’s security policy forbids the page from sending data
      anywhere, so the tool keeps working once the page is loaded, even offline. You can check it in your browser’s
      developer tools: the network tab stays empty while the image is read. <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a>
      and the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> say more.
    </p>
    <p>
      To see which tools write which fields, read the <a href="/guides/what-metadata-do-ai-images-contain/">guide to AI
      image metadata</a>. For phone photos, see <a href="/remove-photo-metadata-gps/">photo metadata and GPS</a>; for
      provenance data, <a href="/remove-c2pa-content-credentials/">C2PA Content Credentials</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="sources">
      Sources: IPTC <a href="https://cv.iptc.org/newscodes/digitalsourcetype/">Digital Source Type vocabulary</a>;
      <a href="https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.2/specs/C2PA_Specification.html">C2PA 2.2
      specification</a>; AUTOMATIC1111
      <a href="https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui/blob/master/modules/images.py">modules/images.py</a>;
      ComfyUI <a href="https://github.com/comfyanonymous/ComfyUI/blob/master/nodes.py">nodes.py</a> and
      <a href="https://github.com/comfyanonymous/ComfyUI/blob/master/comfy/cli_args.py">comfy/cli_args.py</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
